In article <1992Jan29.061623.58395@yuma.acns.colostate.edu> barreraa@lamar.ColoState.EDU (Azael Barrera) writes:
>Hi, DEC users:
>Does anybody in net land has an old copy of BSD that may run in a
>veteran PDP 11/23+, e.g. 2.xBSD Unix?
>If somebody has a copy, even covered by a bookshelf of dust,
>and willing to get rid of it, reply e-mail to the addresses below.

	I'm posting rather than e-mailing since this topic/request comes
	up fairly frequently.

	2BSD is only available as a source distribution, a AT&T Unix
	source license is still required (they're probably worried that
	pdp-11s will once again rule the Unix world) - there's no
	"hobbyist" class license that i am aware of.

	The last 2.xBSD which purported to run on a non-I/D machine was
	2.9BSD.  I never saw 2.9 run on anything smaller than a 11/44.
	Current releases (2.11BSD release almost a year ago) won't fit
	on a 23+.  This is mostly from lack of time and effort, many folks
	want it but no one's offered to help do it.

	Even V7 (which many, but not myself, consider to be the last _real_
	Unix) just _barely_ ran on a 11/23, we had to hack in kernel overlays 
	even then, and it wasn't a pretty sight.   V6 would stand a better
	chance of fitting into a 23 or 23+ but i doubt you'll find a copy
	of that around these days.

	Your best best would be to upgrade the processor with a KDJ-11A
	(11/73) or KDJ-11D (11/53) card and obtain 2.11BSD.

	Steven Schultz
	sms@wlv.imsd.contel.com (tdy: sms@lonex.rl.af.mil)


