Some thoughts:
	- Do you have general purpose workstations and/or turnkey 
	  configurations?
		- Some CAD shops may have workstations dedicated for
		  the use of a particular software package.

	- Does your organization buy all/some workstations?
		- Some support groups may only deal with the machines
		  they buy. Some areas take on "all" systems.

	- Does your organization buy all/some software?
		- Again, similar to the hardware side of things.

	- Do you monitor system "usage" (e.g. cpu, disk, io)?

	- Do you have an algorithm that you use to calculate support
	  for "new" customers?

The idea is to look at how the "old" IS mainframe support was/is done.
Take into account hardware, software, and labor costs to run the shop.
Use an accounting package to monitor the systems, and come up with a 
cost factor that lets you break even at the end of the year.

Basically, if you are a support area, how do you determine staffing
requirements, cost for the staff, etc. I would also be interested in
the demographics, and staff size (there is a lot of discussion as to
the ratio of workstations to system administrator)



