Funny you should mention this, as I've been iside RL dirves quite a bit
recently. But,  I don't beleive it can be done. There are 4 things that
have to be changed : 
1) A link is fitted to the RL02 drive logic board in an RL02, removed for
an RL01. This effectively shifts the cylinder address difference bits so
that an RL02 has 512 tracks, and RL01 has 256 tracks. That could be
controlled by a switch.
2) A link is shifted on the read/write board. This connects a different
preset to the amplifier to set the gain. . I'm not sure why DEC fitted
this, as it is a good idea to check the gain after changing the heads or
R/W board anyway. I wouldn't want to extend that to a switch - I guess the
amplifier woul go unstable. But, as the heads are diffeerent, I may not be
necessary. It may be that this link is to compensate for differences in
the head, rather than # tracks.
3) A link is shifted on the DC servo baord (PSU/head control amplifier).
This could probably be extended to a switch.
4) The heads are different. The RL02 heads record a narrower track (rather
like 40/80 track floppy heads). My guess is that RL02 heads can read, but
not write reliably, RL01 disks

So, if the link mentioned in 1) is switched, and possibly 3 as well, you
may get a drive, which like the 1.2Mbyte drives on PCs can read
low-density disks. But it is not reliable. 

If you want to mail me to chat about the RL drives, please do....
-tony 'PDP11 Hacker' Duell
ard@siva.bris.ac.uk



