                   ALPHA PUBLIC STATEMENTS TO DATE

The following "Alpha Public Statements to Date" were delivered at the
DECUS Alpha Update Session on Friday December 10th, 1991:


TECHNOLOGY
~~~~~~~~~~
-  Advanced CMOS chip custom designed and manufactured by Digital

-  25-year design life, scales in performance by factor of 1,000

-  64-bit, 200 MHz

-  Gracefully allows multiple processors to play together in parallel

-  NOT a VAX.  The only similarities are byte addressing (little
   endian), VAX floating-point, and two instructions: 32-bit load-store 
   and 32-bit add-subtract-multiply.  Uses about 150 instructions.

-  Most closely resembles Cray-1, but is substantially faster

-  Began work on technology in fall of 1988

-  Will support VMS and OSF/1 at initial product rollout; built to
   support lots of operating systems and languages

-  Translator at first release, clusters shortly thereafter (within
   6-8 months)

-  Same hardware boots VMS or OSF, but no need to run both
   simultaneously

-  Longword and quadword granularity

-  IEEE single and double FP support, but no H FP support (translation
   support of H, but no native H support)

-  C, FORTRAN, and ADA compilers at first release

-  Unix BSD kernel ported in 1990, 64-bit OSF/1 port underway now

		==========================================

STRATEGIES
~~~~~~~~~~
-  Across all platforms, desktop to datacenter, including PCs

-  Architecture engineered to support palmtops to supercomputers

-  Looking at licensing the technology

-  Revolutionary within the industry; 21st century computing; beyond
   the RISC curve 

-  Multiple third-party applications ready by initial rollout
-  Industry leading performance and price/performance
	-- Another factor of 3-4 beyond today's 83 MHz VAX products
	-- Clock speed will at least double

-  Investment protection for data, applications, interfaces,
   peripherals; no decision yet on board swap or system level upgrade

-  SMP and cluster versions; includes mixed cluster support (VAX &
   Alpha)

		===========================================

TIMING
~~~~~~
-  Phased introduction over about two years beginning late 1992 or
   early 1993
	-- Working to have several products at initial rollout;
	   one a low-end porting platform

-  VMS port 90 percent complete; rest within six weeks

-  Plan to have all of today's VMS layered products ported by 1994

-  Targeting hundreds of third-party applications by initial rollout
	
-  Today Digital has a dozen Alpha workstations and four or five dozen
   systems at 200 MHz for our software developers to use (breadboard
   prototypes)

-  We will have more to publicly say about Alpha in the February 1992
   timeframe

		===========================================

LICENSING  
~~~~~~~~~
-  While both OSF/1 and VMS will be available at FRS, the architecture
   of Alpha is inherently designed to support other operating systems.

-  As noted above, Digital is looking at licensing the technology

		===========================================

ISSCC ABSTRACT - Paper will be given Feb. 20, 1992
                       
A 200 MHz 64b Dual-Issue CMOS Microprocessor

  "A 400MIPS (peak) custom 64b VLSI CPU on a 16.8x13.9mm2 chip in a
3.3V 0.75micron CMOS technology contains 1/68M transistors and
dissipates 30W at 200 MHz.  The chip includes 8kBI and D caches, a
pipelined FPU and can dual issue among scoreboarded Interger,
Floating-Point, Load/Store and Branch Execution Units."
  

                    BACKGROUND FOR EDITORS AND ANALYSTS
                 A COMPUTING ARCHITECTURE FOR THE 21st CENTURY
    
    
    At the bottom of every computing solution is a set of rules about how 
    data is addressed and instructions are handled.  A set of rules that is 
    the foundation for how a computer -- the hardware that performs 
    operations -- the compiler that turns software instructions into 
    hardware operations -- the operating environment for applications and 
    applications development -- actually works.
    
    It's called a "computing architecture."  And to the extent that a 
    computing architecture is robust and long-lived -- spanning a large 
    range of systems, many kinds of applications, and multiple generations 
    of technology -- it delivers important benefits.  Transitions to new 
    systems and new technologies are smooth and non-disruptive. Investments 
    in hardware, software, and data are protected.  Operational and support 
    requirements are simplified and the overall cost of ownership is 
    lowered. 
    
    Throughout the history of computing there have been many different 
    computing architectures, optimized for many different kinds of 
    technology and applications.  Some span only one system and last only 
    for one generation.  Others, like IBM's System 360 architecture have 
    delivered a consistent computing environment for more than two decades.  
    Still others, like Digital's VAX architecture are able to incorporate 
    new performance technologies (clusters, integrated vector processing, 
    and multiprocessing) -- to last more than a decade -- and to span a 
    broad range of systems.
    
    
    DOES THE WORLD NEED ANOTHER COMPUTING ARCHITECTURE?
    
    Even the best computing architectures, however, don't last forever.  In 
    fact, the most popular computer systems in the world today, both 
    complex instruction set computers (CISC) and reduced instruction set 
    computers (RISC) share an underlying limitation in their architectures 
    that make the development of a new computing architecture inevitable.  
    They are all 32-bit systems.
    
    While systems based on both CISC and RISC 32-bit architectures will 
    continue to deliver leading-edge performance for years to come, they 
    will not be able to keep pace with improvements in memory technology to 
    meet the demands of the "supercomputing-like" applications of the 21st 
    century, including: visualization, very large databases, imaging, 
    multimedia, simulation and modeling.
    
    Quite simply, the 32-bit computing architectures the world uses today 
    are going to run out of address space.  
    
    
    WHAT ARE THE CHARACTERISTICS OF A 21ST CENTURY COMPUTING ARCHITECTURE?
    
    Given that most computer users face the prospect of choosing a new 
    computing architecture sometime within the next decade, what are the 
    characteristics of a computing architecture that will last, that will 
    carry them well into the 21st century?
    
    A careful analysis of past architectures, as well as a projection of 
    current technology trends, yields a number of core characteristics that 
    a computing architecture must have to be viable into the 21st century. 
    
    The following chart shows how both the goals and implementation of 
    computing architectures have changed over time.
    
    








