In article <1992Jan27.164911.1@acvax.inre.asu.edu>, agsja@acvax.inre.asu.edu writes:

|>I have a DECstation 5000/240 but I have certain software applications
|>that require 8-bit colors.  Can anyone tell me how to make the 24-bit
|>machine emulate an 8-bit machine?

Start the X server with the -class PseudoColor option.  If you're running
DECwindows, edit /etc/ttys.  The X server startup is the line that begins
with ":0" --- usually the last line.  It probably looks something like
this:

:0 "/usr/bin/login -P /usr/bin/Xprompter -C /usr/bin/dxsession -e" none on secur
e window="/usr/bin/Xtm"

Add the option to the invocation of the X server:

:0 "/usr/bin/login -P /usr/bin/Xprompter -C /usr/bin/dxsession -e" none on secur
e window="/usr/bin/Xtm -class PseudoColor"

Write back the ttys file and either reboot the machine or do a "kill -1 1".
The latter will have the effect of causing init to restart the X server.

Have fun.

						Frank


