terry@spcvxb.spc.edu (Terry Kennedy, Operations Mgr.) writes:
>   Yes, the DEC "Corporate Mouse" (known around here as the "Corporate Hockey
> Puck") causes problems for some users because their hands are too small to
> easily manipulate the large mouse.
> 
>   Before DEC won an industrial design award (really!) for the current mouse,
> they used a mouse made by (I think, this is from really old memories) a firm
> called Hawley/Mouse House. It was a minor conversion from a Xerox workstation
> mouse (in fact, the model number is something like XEROX backwards). DEC used
> these on the original VAXstations. Perhaps your DEC office could locate one
> given this information?

There is one minor problem -- the old "mouse" (more like a brick on
wheels) has a quite different connector (a D15), which connects by means
of a *long* cord to the distribution panel of a VCB01 graphics board, as
found on the original VAXstation II (*not* II/GPX), and presumably
various other failed graphics systems.  The VCB01 will also accept a
corporate hockey puck plugged into the monitor connection; this overrides
the brick). 

I replaced by brick with a hockey puck some time ago, and will not go
back to the brick -- the ball is metal, and the mouse rides on the ball
by means of a swivelling roller.  If you run the mouse in a circle, the
pointer's movement basically describes a square.  If you put it on a
smooth surface, the pointer won't move all as the ball just slides.  The
thing is not optomechanical -- the roller against the ball drive wheels
with contacts with fine (and easily damaged) brushes.  It's quite heavy,
the buttons (IMHO) are small and badly placed, the cable is stiff, and it
qualifies as the single worst mouse I have ever seen, in just about every
respect.

I still have the horrible thing if anyone wants to take it out of my
sight....  If you really must get one (note I didn't say "buy" -- they
should pay you to take it away) the part "number" is VS10X-EA.

