In article <1992Jan10.234037.19422@mtholyoke.edu> jbotz@mtholyoke.edu (Jurgen Botz) writes:

   About half the software I've compiled under Ultrix 4.x has had problems
   with DEC's "time.h".  Often I get 'conflicting types for "time"' errors,
   but I've gotten others as well.  I can usually fix it by temporarily
   commenting something in DEC's header out.  But seeing as that so much
   software has problems there, does anyone know what DEC is doing wrong,
   and/or can suggest a more permanent fix?  TIA.

'Old' software uses type long for time, current standard is time_t.
DEC has changed its sys/time.h to use time_t, which conflicts with older
software. 

My personal solution is to change, in sys/time.h (actually
/usr/local/lib/gcc-include/sys/time.h since I only use GCC) :

  extern time_t		time( time_t *__tloc );

to:

  #ifdef TIME_T_LONG
  extern long		time( time_t *__tloc );
  #else
  extern time_t		time( time_t *__tloc );
  #endif

When I encounter software that uses 'long time', I only have to supply
-DTIME_T_LONG on the command line.

	Johan
