Steve,

I am just guessing, but it could be that you do not have the fonts installed
that dxcalendar is looking for.   Or you may not have installed the proper
"apps-default" file in /usr/lib/X11/app-defaults (DXcalendar)

"Segmentation fault" on Ultrix/MIPS systems usually means that a zero-value
pointer was dereferenced.  Just the type of thing to happen if the application
was trying to access a string that did not exist.

You can try setting the font resource for dxcalendar to one of your existing
fonts, or make a symbolic link to an existing font.  The fonts it uses are
listed in the app-defaults file.

maddog


