Organization: Edinburgh University
Lines: 52

We intend to purchase a colour PostScript printer for use in a mixed
environment dominated by VMS; our budget limits us to the lower end of
the marketplace.  We would appreciate your advice: recommendations, 
brickbats, war stories, whatever.  Some details of the target environment
follow.

Please respond by e-mail; I will summarise to the net as appropriate.

Thanks in advance

Russ Evans, British Geological Survey, Edinburgh
e_gs18@va.nmh.ac.uk

===========================================================================

Gory details:

Our departmental computing facilities comprise around fifteen VAXstations,
mostly model 3100's with small local storage disks, clustered to our 
computing service 6410, all running VMS 5.4.  Most of the system manage-
ment and operations are conducted for us by the computing service staff.  
We also have around a dozen IBM-PC clones, mostly 286sx's or 386's, with 
Ethernet cards and Pathworks capability.  Our printers are housed in a 
central location, fed through a DECserver 200 running LAT.  We foresee 
some migration towards Unix, and to that end have a single DECstation 
running Ultrix 4.2.  We also have one Mac SE, networked using Pathworks 
and directly connected to the Ethernet.  DECnet/LAT is presently the 
principal protocol suite on our network, but IP activity is also supported
by our computer service and is growing rapidly.  The central VAX server,
which manages the print queues, runs UCX.

Much of our graphics work involves the printing of images generated as 
bitmaps by products such as DEC GKS, integrated with PostScript text.  
Our experience with PostScript printers to date is that they work
extremely slowly as a consequence of sending bitmaps across the serial 
link between the server and the printer, and we would like to avoid this
bottleneck with our next purchase.  We would prefer a printer with
A3 (16x10) capability, if available.

At least one of the products offered to us (Seiko) has a SCSI interface,
and it has been suggested that we could connect this printer to the 
relevant bus on one of our VAXstations, which would then service the 
relevant queue.  We do not find this solution particularly attractive, 
partly because we are unsure of what difficulties we might meet in this 
route, and partly because it will place undesirable constraints on the 
physical location of some of our equipment.  We would like to hear from 
anyone who has attempted to connect a printer to a VAXstation's SCSI bus.  
We would also be interested in any experiences with QMS colour printers.

Our ideal solution would be a colour print server, connected directly to
the Ethernet using LAT.  We are not aware of such a solution, at least
within our price range.  If you know better, please let us know!


