In article <1992Jan8.010910.6590@eco.twg.com> reece@eco.twg.com (Reece R. Pollack) writes:
>In article <1992Jan7.210937.2610@gmuvax2.gmu.edu>, jkwajee@gmuvax2.gmu.edu
>(James A. Kolodziej) writes:
>|>We are running an application using Vermont Creative Software's windows
>|>for data package.  When we are doing menus (versus forms) the cursor still
>|>appears somewhere in the menu (i.e. blinking line).  Although the cursor
>|>being there does not impair our applications, aesthetically, we'd like to
>|>make the cursor disappear while the menu is up.  Can anyone out there
>|>advise us how to achieve this goal?

>|>(We kinda think there should be some sort of escape sequence to send to the 
>|>vt100 to do this.  *any* solution is welcomed though.)

Turn the terminal off, turn the brightness _way_ down, pull the plug,
don't look at the terminal, throw the terminal out the window.  Those
are about the only ways to get rid of the cursor on a VT100 series
terminal.  I don't think that the high-end VT100 (125, 180) support it
either, but I'm not sure.  Hiding the cursor is possible on the VT2nn
and later (VT3nn, VT4nn).


>
>It's got nought to do with Unix, but *some* of the DEC VTxxx series terminals
>support this capability. The control sequence to do it is properly known as
>DECTCEM, or "Text Cursor Enable Mode". I sincerely doubt a true vanilla VT100
>supports this mode.
>
>To set this mode (visible cursor):
>	CSI ? 25 h
>
>To reset this mode (invisible cursor):
>	CSI ? 25 l
>
>Of course, for you 7-bit folks, the CSI character can be replaced with:
>	ESC [

Yup, those'll work.  Note that the last character in the second
sequence is a lower-case "L".

Escape sequences can be your friends!  Honest!  On a VT100, try this
one:  <ESC>[2;8y   (these people are professionals: don't try this at
home!)   Or <ESC>#8  if you like "E".

