Subject: Interest in availability of binaries for packages like TeX?
Message-ID: <1992Jan10.201853.14929@coe.montana.edu>
Organization: Montana State University, College of Engineering, Bozeman MT, 59717
Date: Fri, 10 Jan 1992 20:18:53 GMT



I've been pondering the problems that people have building various packages,
TeX, X11, perl, you-name-it...

Usually after a new version, or patch comes out, there's a flurry of postings
of people requesting help getting the various packages to compile/run/install
on their machines.

Would there be interest on the parts of harried admins, if some of the common
packages were made available pre-compiled, and pretty much ready to plug
and play?

I've been experimenting with various methods of packaging up files for
distribution across our campus, and the method I'm using seems to work OK.

If I tar up the whole mess, provide a simple Makefile to do the installation
of the various links and such, and made it available for FTP for various
architectures, would that be useful?

For example, I have TeX packaged up and ready to go... (except I haven't
moved it into the FTP area...), it's been running here for quite some
time.  Included is dvips547 and metafont.  All the admin has to do is
uncompress it, untar it into some directory, Edit a makefile with the
symbolic link info in it, type make, and 'voila...  Instant TeX.
I was thinking of bundling the changed files from the standard distribution
in a seperate tar file, so people can re-compile if they're really
worried about viri and stuff.

Of course, no warranty can be made as to the suitability of anything related
to this to any purpose whatsoever, other than bandwidth consumption, but
I don't know if it'd be useful.

I realize that various sites manage their file system trees in different ways,
but I think most problems that arose could be solved with symlinks.  Those
