With the DR11W and DRV11-WA boards (and evidently the MDB clone) the
direction of data transfer is entirely controlled by the external
hardware, not by anything in the QIO call to the driver. That is,
READVBLK and WRITEVBLK calls to XADRIVER are virtually identical, once
memory protection checks are made. So the handshaking of read requests
should look quite different to that for write requests. Does it? Please
forgive me if I have just stated something you already know perfectly
well.

We built special hardware here to interface an I2S model 75 image
processor to a DRV11-WA (destined for a VAXstation 3200 but currently
residing on a uVAX II), precisely to avoid buying a dedicated MDB board
and expensive I2S GPIT interface. We also developed our own driver here
to give us some extra capabilities not available with the standard
XADRIVER. If you think our experiences here might be useful to you, drop
me a line.

David.
