What do you want in a development environment besides a compiler
and debugger?  Well, ok, a linker & some libraries would be nice.
All of these are available from GNU (Right now the C++ doesn't
actually work all that well on DECstations, but 2.0 is within
weeks of release and will fix that).

Emacs provides a very nice windowing front-end for both the com-
piler and debugger.  You enter the compiler command and a second
window listing any error message is displayed, then a single key
will take you to the place in the source where the error was.
Similarly, with the debugger... run under GNU emacs you can single-
step through your source and emacs will display a little arrow
before the current line.  Etc., etc.

Admittedly the learning curve is higher than with something like
Turbo-C++, but the functionality is all there and then some.

Also from GNU: Revision control system (rcs 5.6), full
C++ library, various other libraries, and better portability
than you'll get from anything else.

Also the GNU compilers generate excellent code, and tend to have
fewer bugs than commercial compilers. 

To see what all you can get from the FSF's GNU project, ftp to
"prep.ai.mit.edu" and cd to "/pub/gnu".
